  "power_order": "Powers: Pog < Gom < Jop < Zok < Lod",
  "instruction_pages": [
    {
      "body": "In this experiment, we’d like you to pretend that you are an anthropologist studying an alien group called the Numerons. Your job is to figure out some aspects of the Numerons’ number language.\n\nBelow are some things you’ve already discovered about their number language. Please study them carefully.",
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "body": "1. There are two kinds of one-word number words in Numerons’ number language: Digits and Powers.",
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "body": "Digits are words for small numbers. They represent any numeral word up to the value of the base number. In English, they could be any of the numbers below ten (one, two,…, nine). However, the base of the Numerons’ number language is unknown and may not conform to your own number base.\n\nSo far, we have only discovered two Digits: “Fim” and “Miv”. It is unknown whether Fim is greater than Miv or vice versa. They are just two of possibly many more Digits.",
      "notes": "Digits: Fim ? Miv (“?” means the relative magnitude of the two Digits is unknown)"
    },
    {
      "body": "Powers are words for some special large numbers. They represent a category of mainly nounlike numeral forms often used as multiplicational components. They are like thousand, billion, and other similar kinds of number words in English. Remember, the base of the Numerons’ number language is unknown and may not conform to your own number base.\n\nSo far, we have discovered five powers: “Pog,” “Gom,” “Jop,” “Zok,” and “Lod.” Lod is greater than Zok, Zok is greater than Jop, Jop is greater than Gom, and Gom is greater than Pog. These are just five of possibly many more multipliers.",
      "notes": "Powers: Pog < Gom < Jop < Zok < Lod"
    },
    {
      "body": "2. Number words in the Numeron language refer to positive whole numbers.",
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "body": "3. The Numerons’ number words are made by using a set of one-word number words and combining them to produce more complex numbers.",
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "body": "4. The number words are words used in the Numerons’ natural language (like “four” or “seventy-two” in English). They are NOT number symbols, such as the Arabic or Roman numerals (such as “4,” “IV,” “72,” or “LXXII”).",
      "notes": ""
    }
  ],
  "comprehension_intro": "Below are some questions to check your understanding of the Numeron's language.\n\nUse the number keys to select your answer or answers, then press Right Arrow to submit.",
